# kext-vtable-padding: the kexts this kernel loads come from a Mac kernelcache, whose classes carry
# OSMetaClassDeclareReservedUnused() slots. XNU emits those only for XNU_TARGET_OS_OSX, and this
# kernel builds as PLATFORM=iPhoneOS, so every virtual method after the first padding block sits at
# a lower vtable index here than in the kext's headers. AppleImage4 then calls
# obj->vtable[0x2d8] expecting IORegistryEntry::getProperty and lands on IOService::handleOpen,
# whose signing salt differs, so the authenticated call fails:
#   "PAC failure from kernel with IA key while branching to X8" at pc 0xfffffff009581910.
# Measured on 25F84: 296 of 298 kernel classes order their virtuals identically to Apple's, 150 are
# index-identical once the padding is dropped and 146 differ only by insertions.
# Only correct while the loaded collection is a Mac collection: an iPad collection expects padding
# off, so this patch belongs to the macOS profiles and must not be shared with an iOS series.
kext-vtable-padding.patch

# fileset-wait-probe: a kext waiting for a service this kernel never publishes produces no output at
# all (waitForService(matching, NULL) becomes UINT64_MAX). AppleImage4 blocks exactly that way after
# its nonce magazine reports "failed to read nonce slot data", because it waits for "IONVRAM" and
# "IOAESAccelerator" and neither exists in this collection. Inert unless the boot args are passed:
#   fileset-wait-log=1        print each request (provider class + timeout) before the wait
#   fileset-wait-cap-ms=N     cap an unbounded wait at N ms so the caller takes its own error path
fileset-wait-probe.patch

# publish-ionvram-resource: AppleImage4 waits with no timeout for the "IONVRAM" resource
# (resourceMatching("IONVRAM") = IOResources + IOResourceMatch). Apple publishes that resource from
# IOPlatformExpert::registerNVRAMController() when a platform NVRAM controller registers; this kernel
# creates its IODTNVRAM from the device tree's /options node but never registers a controller, so the
# resource never appears and the wait never returns. Publishing it where the IODTNVRAM instance is
# registered claims exactly what is true and lets the kext continue.
publish-ionvram-resource.patch

# lck-gate-probe: B14 still stopped with the same 12 nonce-magazine lines and printed no
# fileset-wait: line at all, so AppleImage4 never reached a waitForService. In that function
# (0x958922c) the calls right after the magazine read are IOLockLock / lck_mtx_gate_close /
# IOLockUnlock, and both a stuck mutex and gate_ilock() (a spin on the gate's interlock bit with no
# timeout) hang silently. This prints at the entry of every lck_mtx_gate_* call and on every
# contended lck_mtx_lock, so one boot says which of the two it is.
lck-gate-probe.patch

# uke-heartbeat: a stuck kext and a console that stopped refreshing look identical on the panel,
# which is the ambiguity B12..B15 kept hitting (AppleImage4 prints twelve nonce-magazine lines and
# then nothing changes). Two seconds apart, a thread call prints "uke-heartbeat <n>": if those keep
# appearing the kernel still runs and only one thread is blocked, and if they stop the console itself
# stopped updating.
uke-heartbeat.patch
